\begin{center}
{
\Large
Sorting Quiz Practice Problems
}
\end{center}

\begin{note}
A \emph{sorting algorithm} takes a list
$x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n$ of elements and returns a permutation of the
elements that is in increasing order. Sorting is the canonical
\emph{divide and conquer} problem: both of the algorithms in this
section, \emph{merge sort} and \emph{quick sort}, split the input
into smaller pieces, recursively sort each piece, and then combine
the sorted pieces into a single sorted output.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
\emph{Merge sort} splits the input into two halves, recursively
sorts each half, and merges the two sorted halves into a sorted
list. This gives the recurrence
\[
T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + O(n).
\]
By case 2 of the master theorem, $T(n) = O(n \log n)$.
\end{note}

\begin{note}
Every problem below begins with the same unsorted list
\lstinline{[3, 7, 1, 8, 5, 10, 2, 9, 4, 6]}.
The maximum element (10) sits at the middle index 5, and the list is
otherwise in no particular order; both properties matter for the
edge cases that the incorrect implementations trigger.
\end{note}
